# Rank 1112 / CVE-2026-40895: follow-redirects strips only three hardcoded
# header names (`authorization`, `proxy-authorization`, `cookie`) when a
# redirect crosses domains or downgrades protocol, so a custom authentication
# header the application attached -- `X-Api-Key`, `X-Auth-Token` and the like --
# is copied to the next request unchanged and reaches whatever host the 3xx
# pointed at (CWE-200, CWE-212). The report that belongs to this CVE is the
# dependency finding: the fix (844c4d3, released in 1.16.0) adds a
# `sensitiveHeaders` request option and builds the strip pattern from the three
# built-in names plus the caller's list, so the release is the first that can
# strip a caller-named credential header at all. A caller that does not pass
# the option behaves identically before and after the upgrade; the pin below
# 1.16.0 is the necessary condition and the reportable one.
#
# The credential-header redirect family (VYQL-SEC-052) is deliberately not this
# CVE's report: its binding fires on a redirect follower that never names any
# credential header, and this library deletes the three standard ones in every
# revision -- the defect is the headers outside that list, which no code-level
# fact in the library or the application states on its own. The last two tests
# pin that boundary on the library's own vulnerable and fixed forms.

test "rank1112: a follow-redirects pin below the first release that can strip caller-named headers is a dependency finding"
  lang javascript
  file app.js
  code
  ```
  const followRedirects = require('follow-redirects');

  function fetchWithApiKey(url, apiKey) {
    return followRedirects.https.request(url, {
      headers: { 'X-Api-Key': apiKey },
    });
  }

  module.exports = { fetchWithApiKey };
  ```
  file package.json
  code
  ```
  {
    "name": "billing-sync",
    "dependencies": {
      "follow-redirects": "1.15.11"
    }
  }
  ```
  expect VYQL-SCA-001
  expect_evidence VYQL-SCA-001 CVE-2026-40895
  reject VYQL-SEC-052
  reject VYQL-SEC-025

